<<setup, echo=FALSE>>=
options(width = 72, prompt = "R> ", continue = "+  ")
@

\section{What rsimd does}

\pkg{rsimd} computes common operations on R's atomic vectors, such as sums,
elementwise arithmetic, predicates and elementary functions, with the single
instruction, multiple data (SIMD) instructions of x86-64 and arm64 CPUs.
Each function is a counterpart of a base R function, named
\code{simd\_} followed by what it computes, and follows base R's rules for
result types, missing values and warnings, with any differences documented
on its help page. It is not always a drop-in replacement: attributes and
classes are dropped, and only a length-one operand is recycled; the
\emph{Numerical semantics} vignette
(\code{vignette("numerical-semantics", package = "rsimd")}) has the
details. The package detects the CPU's instruction sets when it is
loaded and uses the best implementation it supports, while letting you
choose another one, including plain C, by name.

A SIMD instruction works on a vector register that holds several numbers,
called \emph{lanes}, and applies the same operation to every lane at once:
one instruction adds two doubles in each of the lanes of two registers, where
plain C code adds one pair at a time. How many lanes a register has depends
on the instruction set:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llrrr}
\hline
Implementation & Instruction set & Register bits & Doubles & Integers \\
\hline
\code{sse2} & x86-64 SSE2 & 128 & 2 & 4 \\
\code{avx2} & x86-64 AVX2 with FMA & 256 & 4 & 8 \\
\code{avx512} & x86-64 AVX-512 (F, BW, DQ, VL) & 512 & 8 & 16 \\
\code{neon} & arm64 Advanced SIMD & 128 & 2 & 4 \\
\code{sve}, \code{sve2} & arm64 Scalable Vector Extension & 128--2048 & 2--32 & 4--64 \\
\code{none} & plain C, no SIMD & & 1 & 1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent The SVE register size is chosen by the CPU, not the program, and
the same \pkg{rsimd} code runs at whatever size it finds.

\section{Installation}

Install the released version from CRAN, or the development version from a
copy of its git repository:

<<install, eval=FALSE>>=
install.packages("rsimd")
@

\begin{verbatim}
git clone https://github.com/sjp/rsimd.git
R CMD INSTALL rsimd
\end{verbatim}

\noindent The package has to be compiled, so building it from source needs a
C compiler (on Windows, Rtools). Its \code{configure} script tries each
implementation the platform could have and builds those the compiler
supports; the installation log lists them. On an arm64 Linux machine with
GCC it prints

\begin{verbatim}
rsimd: compiler aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc, target aarch64-linux
checking whether aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc can build the none tier... yes
checking whether aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc can build the neon tier... yes
checking whether the neon tier can use SLEEF (sleefinline_advsimd.h)... yes
checking whether aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc can build the sve tier with
  -march=armv8-a+sve... yes
checking whether aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc can build the sve2 tier with
  -march=armv8-a+sve2... yes
rsimd: building tiers none,neon,sve,sve2
rsimd: tiers with SLEEF elementary functions: neon,sve,sve2
\end{verbatim}

\noindent (some lines left out). On x86-64 the same lines name
\code{sse2}, \code{avx2} and \code{avx512}. Building an implementation does
not mean it will be used: that also needs a CPU that supports it, which is
checked every time the package is loaded. The vignette \emph{Choosing an
implementation} explains both steps.

\section{Your machine}

\code{simd\_cpu\_features()} reports what the package found out about the
CPU; its \code{features} element is a logical vector with one entry for
each instruction set extension \pkg{rsimd} knows about.
\code{simd\_available()} lists the implementations this machine can use,
best first and always ending with \code{"none"}, and \code{simd\_current()}
the one in use. The output below is from the machine that built this
document; yours may differ.

<<machine>>=
library(rsimd)
cpu <- simd_cpu_features()
cpu$arch
names(which(cpu$features))
simd_available()
simd_current()
@

\section{Basic operations}

Every function takes and returns plain R vectors. Results agree with base R
to within rounding, and often exactly:

<<basic>>=
set.seed(1)
x <- runif(1e5)
y <- runif(1e5)
all.equal(simd_sum(x), sum(x))
all.equal(simd_mean(x), mean(x))
all.equal(simd_add(x, y), x + y)
all.equal(simd_fma(x, y, 1), x * y + 1)
all.equal(simd_exp(x), exp(x))
@

Missing values follow base R. \code{simd\_is\_na()} is \code{is.na()},
\code{na.rm} works as in \code{sum()}, and a length-one operand is used for
every element:

<<basic-na>>=
v <- c(1.5, NA, 4, NaN)
simd_is_na(v)
simd_sum(v)
simd_sum(v, na.rm = TRUE)
simd_add(v, 10)
simd_mul(1:4, 2L)
@

Integer and logical vectors give integer results where base R's operators
do, and the functions work on raw, complex and \pkg{bit64}
\code{integer64} vectors where that makes sense; each help page lists the
types it takes. A broader set of examples by family:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.68\textwidth}}
\hline
Family & Functions (help page) \\
\hline
Reductions & \code{simd\_sum}, \code{simd\_mean}, \code{simd\_min},
  \code{simd\_max}, \code{simd\_max\_abs}, \code{simd\_which\_max},
  \code{simd\_which\_max\_abs}, \code{simd\_prod\_sums},
  \code{simd\_any\_na}, \code{simd\_count\_na}, \code{simd\_which},
  \code{simd\_count} (\code{?simd\_sum}, \code{?simd\_min},
  \code{?simd\_which\_min}, \code{?simd\_any\_na}) \\
Statistics & \code{simd\_dot}, \code{simd\_norm}, \code{simd\_dist},
  \code{simd\_var}, \code{simd\_sd}, \code{simd\_cumsum}
  (\code{?simd\_dot}, \code{?simd\_var}, \code{?simd\_cumsum}) \\
Arithmetic & \code{simd\_add}, \code{simd\_mul}, \code{simd\_idiv},
  \code{simd\_fma}, \code{simd\_pmin}, \code{simd\_round}, the
  \code{\_wrap} forms (\code{?simd\_add}, \code{?simd\_fma},
  \code{?simd\_pmin}, \code{?simd\_round}) \\
Predicates, logic & \code{simd\_is\_na}, \code{simd\_is\_finite},
  \code{simd\_is\_whole}, \code{simd\_is\_pow2}, \code{simd\_eq},
  \code{simd\_lt}, \code{simd\_hamming}, \code{simd\_and},
  \code{simd\_any} (\code{?simd\_is\_na}, \code{?simd\_eq},
  \code{?simd\_hamming}, \code{?simd\_and}) \\
Bits & \code{simd\_bit\_and}, \code{simd\_shl}, \code{simd\_popcount}
  (\code{?simd\_bit\_and}, \code{?simd\_popcount}) \\
Conversions & \code{simd\_as\_double}, \code{simd\_as\_integer},
  \code{simd\_as\_integer64} (\code{?simd\_as\_integer}) \\
Elementary functions & \code{simd\_exp}, \code{simd\_log},
  \code{simd\_exp2m1}, \code{simd\_log1p}, \code{simd\_sin},
  \code{simd\_sinpi}, \code{simd\_sincospi}, \code{simd\_tanh},
  \code{simd\_pow}, \code{simd\_rootn} (\code{?simd\_exp}, \code{?simd\_sin},
  \code{?simd\_sinh}, \code{?simd\_sinpi}, \code{?simd\_pow}) \\
Floating point & \code{simd\_ilogb}, \code{simd\_scaleb},
  \code{simd\_next\_up}, \code{simd\_remainder} (\code{?simd\_ilogb});
  approximations \code{simd\_recip\_approx}, \code{simd\_rsqrt\_approx}
  (\code{?simd\_recip\_approx}) \\
Machine learning & \code{simd\_sigmoid}, \code{simd\_softmax},
  \code{simd\_log\_softmax} (\code{?simd\_softmax}) \\
Control & \code{simd\_use}, \code{simd\_with\_impl},
  \code{simd\_precision} (\code{?simd\_use}, \code{?simd\_precision},
  \code{?rsimd\_options}) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{The simd\_vec wrapper}

Calling \code{simd\_add(x, y)} everywhere is verbose. A \code{simd\_vec} is
an ordinary vector with a class whose operators, \code{Math} and
\code{Summary} methods and \code{mean()} call the \pkg{rsimd} functions:

<<vec>>=
a <- simd_vec(c(1, 4, 9, 16))
a
b <- sqrt(a) + 1
b
sum(b)
mean(a)
a > 5
@

Values are wrapped again, while reductions, comparisons and predicates
return plain values. \code{as.vector()} or \code{as.double()} unwraps.

A \code{simd\_vec} can be \emph{pinned} to one implementation. It then runs
there whatever the global selection is, and results keep the pin; this is
handy for comparing implementations or keeping one part of a program on
plain C. Operands pinned to different implementations are an error:

<<vec-pin>>=
p <- simd_vec(c(1, 2, 3), impl = "none")
simd_impl(p)
simd_impl(p * 2)
q <- simd_vec(c(1, 2, 3), impl = simd_available()[1])
msg <- tryCatch(p + q, error = conditionMessage)
writeLines(strwrap(msg, 70))
simd_impl(q) <- NULL
p + q
@

A \code{simd\_vec} also remembers whether it is known to contain no missing
values. \code{check\_na = TRUE} looks once; afterwards the functions skip
their missing-value checks on it, and results that cannot have gained a
missing value keep the flag:

<<vec-na>>=
f <- simd_vec(runif(10), check_na = TRUE)
simd_na_free(f)
simd_na_free(abs(f))
simd_na_free(f / 0)
anyNA(f)
as.vector(f[1:3])
@

\code{?simd\_vec} has the complete rules.

\section{When to expect a speed-up}

SIMD helps when a loop over the data does enough work per element and the
vectors are long enough. Three effects decide what you see; the vignette
\emph{Benchmarks} has measured numbers.

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Call overhead.} Each call goes from R into C, checks its
  arguments and selects a kernel, which costs a few microseconds. For
  vectors of a thousand elements this is most of the time, and base R's
  built-in operators, which have less to do per call, are often faster.
\item \emph{Memory-bound operations.} Elementwise arithmetic such as
  \code{simd\_add()} reads two vectors and writes one. Once the vectors are
  larger than the CPU caches, memory bandwidth limits both base R and
  \pkg{rsimd}, and the gain over base R is small. A SIMD implementation is
  still clearly faster than \code{"none"}.
\item \emph{Compute-bound operations.} Sums, dot products, comparisons,
  conversions and predicates over long vectors do little memory traffic per
  operation and gain most: several times faster than base R is common.
  Elementary functions such as \code{simd\_exp()} are compute-bound too,
  but their gain depends on how fast the platform's C math library already
  is.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Results never depend on speed: every implementation is tested
against \code{"none"}, which is plain C, and the help pages state where
results may differ in the last bits.

\section{Complex numbers}

R stores a complex vector as pairs of doubles: the real part, then the
imaginary part. \pkg{rsimd} supports these complex operations:

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{simd\_add()}, \code{simd\_sub()}, \code{simd\_mul()},
  \code{simd\_div()} and \code{simd\_neg()};
\item \code{simd\_sum()}, \code{simd\_prod()}, \code{simd\_mean()},
  \code{simd\_prod\_sums()} and \code{simd\_prod\_diffs()}, in every
  \code{simd\_precision()} mode, and \code{simd\_cumsum()} and
  \code{simd\_cumprod()};
\item \code{simd\_conj()}, \code{simd\_re()}, \code{simd\_im()},
  \code{simd\_abs()} (the modulus, as \code{Mod()}) and \code{simd\_arg()},
  and the largest and smallest moduli and where they are,
  \code{simd\_max\_abs()}, \code{simd\_min\_abs()},
  \code{simd\_which\_max\_abs()} and \code{simd\_which\_min\_abs()};
\item the comparisons \code{simd\_eq()} and \code{simd\_ne()};
\item the predicates \code{simd\_is\_na()}, \code{simd\_is\_nan()},
  \code{simd\_is\_finite()} and \code{simd\_is\_infinite()}, with their
  \code{\_any} and \code{\_all} forms, and \code{simd\_any\_na()},
  \code{simd\_count\_na()} and \code{simd\_which\_na()};
\item \code{simd\_hamming()}, where a pair differs when either part does;
\item the elementary functions base R defines on complex numbers:
  \code{simd\_sqrt()}, \code{simd\_exp()}, \code{simd\_log()} (also with
  a base), \code{simd\_log2()}, \code{simd\_log10()}, \code{simd\_pow()},
  the trigonometric and hyperbolic functions and their inverses, and
  \code{simd\_atan2()}.
\end{itemize}

A double, integer or logical operand of the arithmetic and comparison
functions is converted to complex, and a length-one operand is reused for
every element, as in base R:

<<complex-arith>>=
z <- complex(real = c(1, -2, 3), imaginary = c(0.5, 1, -1))
simd_add(z, 1i)
simd_sub(c(10, 20, 30), z)
simd_conj(z)
simd_re(z)
simd_sum(z)
@

Missing values propagate part by part, so a sum can have a missing real part
and a known imaginary part, exactly as with \code{sum()}. A complex number
is missing for \code{is.na()} when either part is, so \code{na.rm = TRUE}
removes the whole element:

<<complex-na>>=
w <- c(1 + 2i, NA, 3 + 1i)
simd_im(w)
simd_sum(w)
Im(simd_sum(w))
simd_sum(w, na.rm = TRUE)
simd_is_na(complex(real = 1, imaginary = NaN))
@

Multiplication, division, \code{cumsum()} and \code{cumprod()} give exactly
base R's results, including infinities, zeros and the choice between
\code{NA} and \code{NaN}. That takes some care, because base R leaves complex
arithmetic to the C compiler that built it, and compilers differ: one may
fuse a product into the following addition (a fused multiply-add, rounded
once) where another rounds both, and C runtimes divide with different
algorithms. When \pkg{rsimd} loads, base R computes products, quotients and
a \code{cumprod()} of a fixed set of inputs, and \pkg{rsimd} picks the
variant of its kernels that reproduces all of them:

<<complex-variants>>=
attr(simd_current(), "complex")
identical(simd_mul(z, z), z * z)
identical(simd_div(1, z), 1 / z)
simd_abs(z)
simd_eq(z, Conj(z))
@

Where no variant matches (division on macOS, for example), \pkg{rsimd} uses
base R's operator for every element, which is exact but no faster.
\code{simd\_abs()} and \code{simd\_arg()} follow the elementary-function
rules: within a fraction of a unit in the last place, with infinite and
missing parts exactly as base R. \code{simd\_prod()} multiplies with base
R's \code{*} in an order set by the precision mode, and the
\code{"compensated"} mode is usually as accurate as base R's
\code{long double} product. The other comparisons (\code{<} and friends)
are an error, as in base R.

The elementary functions follow a two-level contract. The \code{"none"}
implementation calls what base R calls (the C library's \code{csqrt},
\code{cexp}, \code{clog} and so on, and base R's own code for powers,
\code{tan}, the inverse functions on their branch cuts, logarithms with a
base and \code{atan2}), so its results are identical to base R's. The SIMD
implementations compute elements with finite parts from SLEEF's real
functions, within 2 to 5 units in the last place of the exact value (the
bound of each function is in \code{?simd\_exp}; the C library's own complex
functions are within about 5), and leave infinite and missing parts, base
R's branch-cut code and the few regions their formulas do not handle to the
\code{"none"} code, so special values and branch cuts are base R's. Powers
with a whole-number exponent (up to 65536) multiply as base R does and are
identical to base R on every implementation:

<<complex-math>>=
simd_exp(1i * pi)
simd_log(c(-1+0i, complex(real = -1, imaginary = -0)))
identical(simd_pow(z, 3), z^3)
identical(simd_pow(z, -2), z^-2)
simd_with_impl("none", identical(simd_sqrt(z), sqrt(z)))
max(abs(simd_asin(z) - asin(z)) / Mod(asin(z)))
@

The one deliberate difference is \code{simd\_acosh()}: base R computes
\code{acosh(z)} as \code{acos(z) * 1i}, which has a negative real part in
the lower half-plane and on the real axis beyond 1. \pkg{rsimd} returns the
principal value, C's \code{cacosh}:

<<complex-acosh>>=
acosh(c(2 - 1i, 2 + 0i))
simd_acosh(c(2 - 1i, 2 + 0i))
@

\section{Where to go next}

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Choosing an implementation}
  (\code{vignette("implementation-selection", package = "rsimd")}): the
  implementations, how one is chosen and how to choose one yourself, and
  troubleshooting.
\item \emph{Numerical semantics and precision}
  (\code{vignette("numerical-semantics", package = "rsimd")}): missing
  values, the precision modes for sums, integer overflow, and every
  difference from base R.
\item \emph{Benchmarks} (\code{vignette("benchmarks", package = "rsimd")}):
  measured times on real machines and how to run the benchmarks yourself.
\item \code{help(package = "rsimd")} lists every help page;
  \code{?rsimd\_options} lists the options and environment variables.
\end{itemize}

